{"cuisine/malaysian-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/malaysian-chinese-cuisine","n":"Malaysian Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Malaysia","intro":[["Malaysian Chinese cuisine is derived from the culinary traditions of Chinese Malaysian immigrants and their descendants, who have adapted or modified their culinary traditions under the influence of Malaysian culture as well as immigration patterns of Chinese to Malaysia. Malaysian Chinese cuisine is predominantly based on an eclectic repertoire of dishes with roots from Fujian, Cantonese, Hakka and Teochew cuisines."],["As these early immigrants settled in different regions throughout what was then British Malaya and Borneo, they carried with them traditions of foods and recipes that were particularly identified, which gradually became infused with the characteristics of their new home locale in Malaysia while remaining distinctively Chinese. For example, Hainanese chicken rice is usually flavoured with tropical pandan leaves and served with chilli sauce for dipping, and tastes unlike the typical chicken dishes found in Hainan Island itself. Some of these foods and recipes became closely associated with a specific city, town or village, eventually developing iconic status and culminating in a proliferation of nationwide popularity in the present day."],["Chinese food is especially prominent in areas with concentrated Chinese communities, at roadside stalls, hawker centres and kopi tiam, as well as smart cafes and upmarket restaurants throughout the nation. Many Chinese dishes have pork as a component ingredient, but chicken is available as a substitution for Muslim customers from the wider community, and some Chinese restaurants are even halal-certified."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"malaysian-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaysian_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Malaysian Chinese Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"malaysianchinesekitchen.com","note":"Blog dedicated to Malaysian Chinese home cooking, with recipes and videos.","url":"https://www.malaysianchinesekitchen.com/about","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/06/Penangckt.JPG/500px-Penangckt.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Chensiyuan assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penangckt.JPG"}},"ingredient/agar":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/agar","n":"Agar","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Agar ( or ), or agar-agar, is a jelly-like substance consisting of polysaccharides obtained from the cell walls of some species of red algae (phylum Rhodophyta) primarily from the Gracilaria genus (Irish moss, ogonori) and the Gelidiaceae family (tengusa). These algae are known as agarophytes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-agar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Cenil","dish/cenil"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/43/GreenTeaYokan.jpg/500px-GreenTeaYokan.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Sjschen","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GreenTeaYokan.jpg"}},"dish/chicken-katsu":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chicken-katsu","n":"Chicken katsu","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Yoshoku","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chicken katsu, also known as panko chicken or tori katsu (鶏カツ), is a Japanese dish of fried chicken made with panko bread crumbs. It is related to tonkatsu, fried pork cutlets. The dish has spread internationally and has become a common dish served at Japanese and East Asian restaurants worldwide."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chicken-katsu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicken_katsu"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Fried chicken","ingredient/fried-chicken"],["Japanese rice","ingredient/japanese-rice"],["Panko","ingredient/panko"]]},{"h":"More from Yoshoku","links":[["Cream stew","dish/cream-stew"],["Doria (food)","dish/doria-food"],["Hayashi rice","dish/hayashi-rice"],["Menchi-katsu","dish/menchi-katsu"],["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"],["Tonkatsu","dish/tonkatsu"],["Toruko rice","dish/toruko-rice"],["Volga rice","dish/volga-rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e5/Matsunoya_W_Mega_Chicken_Katsu_Set_20200923-04.jpg/500px-Matsunoya_W_Mega_Chicken_Katsu_Set_20200923-04.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"円周率３パーセント","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsunoya_W_Mega_Chicken_Katsu_Set_20200923-04.jpg"}},"ingredient/panko":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/panko","n":"Panko","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Breadcrumbs are a culinary ingredient consisting of flour or crumbled bread of varying dryness, sometimes with seasonings added. They are used for a variety of purposes, including breading or crumbing foods before frying (such as breaded cutlets like tonkatsu and schnitzel), topping casseroles, stuffing poultry, thickening stews, and adding inexpensive bulk to soups, meatloaves, and similar foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-panko"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panko"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Chicken katsu","dish/chicken-katsu"],["Menchi-katsu","dish/menchi-katsu"],["Tonkatsu","dish/tonkatsu"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Fried chicken","ingredient/fried-chicken"],["Japanese rice","ingredient/japanese-rice"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Oyster","ingredient/oyster"],["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Cutlet","ingredient/cutlet"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}]},"ingredient/escabeche":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/escabeche","n":"Escabeche","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Escabeche is the name for several dishes in Spanish, French, Portuguese, Italian, Filipino and Hispanic American cuisines, consisting of marinated fish, meat or vegetables, cooked or pickled in an acidic sauce (usually with vinegar), and flavored with paprika, citrus, and other spices."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-escabeche"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escabeche"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Bolivian cuisine","cuisine/bolivian-cuisine",[["Chola sandwich","dish/chola-sandwich"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pernil","ingredient/pernil"],["Torresmo","ingr